WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.560
We need stories.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.410
We need stories to
understand ourselves.

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.730
We are the only creature
that does this unusual thing

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.548
of telling each other
stories in order

00:00:10.548 --> 00:00:12.840
to try and understand the
kind of creature that we are.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.830
When a child is born, the
first thing the child requires

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.320
is safety and love.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.880
The next thing that the child
asks for is, "Tell me a story."

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.917
I've been writing now
for over 50 years.

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:35.250
And this is the first time
I've done anything like this.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.530
You will learn the
lessons I had to find out

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.890
the hard way-- character,
plot, dialogue, rhythm.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.718
I do think language
has a musicality.

00:00:42.718 --> 00:00:44.760
There's all this stuff
about three-act structure,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.730
exactly how you must
allow a story to unfold.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.890
My view is it's all nonsense.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.290
The first and only
rule is be interesting.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.640
Make it as vivid as you can.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.680
Bam!

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.950
As an exercise, write
300 words about the place

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.570
without using any adjectives.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.680
If you can't say that somewhere
is beautiful or green, what

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.470
it forces you to do is
to tell a story about it.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.570
I'm going to read
from this manuscript I

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.890
wrote in my mid 20s.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.530
It was a failure, so much
so that I never showed it

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.480
to anybody until this.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.630
(The sky is nude today,
encasing his documentary

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.720
in a very un-February
blue.) You see,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.480
I don't even know
what that's about.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.100
I had a very bumpy start.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.980
And to be frank, I
wrote a lot of garbage.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.140
Part of the reason
why I was lost

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.790
is that I had grown
up in one culture

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.050
and I was living in another.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.290
And I didn't quite understand
my relationship with either.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.690
I really needed to
work out who I was,

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.310
because if you're not
clear about who you are,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.220
then you're not clear
about how to write.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.290
Whatever you write,
write because it

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.480
comes from some very
deep place inside you

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.900
that you really, really,
really need to express.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.660
Just make sure that you don't
stop till you get to the end.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:06.360
I'm Salman Rushdie, and
this is my MasterClass.

